---
import Icon from "@/components/Icon.astro";
import Docs from "@/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { docHref } from "@/lib/docs";
import { examples } from "@/lib/examples";
import { DEPLOYED_DEMOS } from "@/lib/site";
---

<Docs
  title="Examples"
  description="Live httpeers examples that run in your browser, and the four deployed demo sites that form one mesh."
>
  <h1>Examples</h1>
  <p>
    Two kinds. The <strong>live examples</strong> run the libraries in this tab: peers, a hub and their
    tokens, joined by MessagePorts instead of libp2p, so they need no relay and nothing leaves your
    browser. The <strong>deployed demos</strong> are four sites on four origins that form one real
    mesh over the relay at <code>relay.httpeers.net</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="live">Live examples</h2>
  <div class="not-prose grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    {
      examples.map((example, i) => (
        <a
          href={`/examples/${example.slug}`}
          class="group rounded-xl border bg-card p-5 no-underline transition-colors hover:border-primary/50"
        >
          <p class="font-mono text-xs text-primary">0{i + 1} · runs in this tab</p>
          <p class="mt-2 font-semibold text-foreground">{example.title}</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">{example.summary}</p>
          <p class="mt-3 inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-primary">
            Open and run <Icon name="arrowRight" class="size-3.5" />
          </p>
        </a>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <h2 id="deployed-demos">Deployed demos</h2>
  <p>
    Each page is its own build because each must be its own origin: the edge that routes mesh calls is a
    ServiceWorker, and a ServiceWorker's scope is an origin. The pages read the relay's address at runtime
    from <code>relay.httpeers.net</code>. Source and details: <a href={docHref("apps/demos/README.md")}>the demo sites</a>.
  </p>
  <div class="not-prose grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    {
      DEPLOYED_DEMOS.map((demo) => (
        <a href={demo.href} class="rounded-xl border bg-card p-5 no-underline transition-colors hover:border-primary/50">
          <p class="flex items-center gap-2 font-semibold text-foreground">
            {demo.title}
            <Icon name="externalLink" class="size-3.5 text-muted-foreground" />
          </p>
          <p class="mt-1 font-mono text-xs text-primary">{demo.href.replace("https://", "").replace(/\/$/, "")}</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">{demo.body}</p>
        </a>
      ))
    }
  </div>
  <h3>Try them</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Open <a href="https://hub.httpeers.net/">hub.httpeers.net</a>. The tab becomes the mesh's hub and mints
      invitations, shown as QR codes.
    </li>
    <li>
      Open <a href="https://images.httpeers.net/">images.httpeers.net</a> (on another device, or in another
      window) and paste or scan the invitation into its join widget. It joins and serves its gallery to the
      mesh; add a picture of your own with the camera or file input.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do the same on <a href="https://app.httpeers.net/">app.httpeers.net</a> with a fresh invitation (each is
      single-use). It finds the images provider and calls it with a bare <code>fetch()</code>. Its
      <strong>Open in a new session</strong> button opens the hub's app in a fresh
      <code>&lt;random&gt;.p.httpeers.net</code> origin that can reach only that peer.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each page's join widget shows how it reaches the hub, <code>Connected (direct)</code> or
      <code>Connected (relay)</code>, and <strong>Leave this mesh…</strong> forgets the origin's identity.
    </li>
  </ol>
</Docs>
